      "side": 0,
      "message": "James,\n\nAs per our understanding, there is a discrepancy between the documentation and the library’s actual capabilities. While the documentation states that input frames must match `g_input_bit_depth`, the library assumes that input frames are as per `g_bit_depth`. This is because library has no implementation for converting input from `g_input_bit_depth` to `g_bit_depth`. It considers input provided is ready for encoding. Hence the current change is performing source validation based on `g_bit_depth`.\n\nIn current version of `aomenc.c` application, if `g_input_bit_depth` \u003e `g_bit_depth`, the application automatically modifies `g_bit_depth` to `g_input_bit_depth` to match the input. Conversely, if `g_input_bit_depth` \u003c `g_bit_depth`, `aomenc.c` manually upshifts the image by (`g_bit_depth - g_input_bit_depth`) bits before passing it to the encoder. This ensures the library receives data already aligned with its expectations (i.e., as per `g_bit_depth`). \n \nOverall, we see the following potential approaches to address this:\n1) Handle cases where g_input_bit_depth !\u003d g_bit_depth inside library. This would mean, `validate_img()` would perform range checks based on `ctx-\u003eoxcf.input_cfg.input_bit_depth`, then execute the necessary bit-depth conversion before encoding. This may require modifications to `aomenc.c` which is currently performing these processes (and any other applications/unit-tests).\n2) Merge current patch + modify the API explanation of g_input_bit_depth and g_bit_depth.\n   g_bit_depth - corresponds to encoding bit-depth and input bit-depth\n   g_input_bit_depth - corresponds to the bit-depth for which quality metrics are computed (for example --psnr\u003d1 as in the current codebase)\n3) Merge current patch + Deprecate/clean up g_input_bit_depth altogether. In this case --psnr\u003d1 should be cleaned-up as well and --psnr\u003d2 becomes default.\n   g_bit_depth - corresponds to encoding bit-depth and input bit-depth\n4) Mandate g_input_bit_depth \u003d g_bit_depth to support legacy API parameters. In this case, current patch may not be required.\n \nThese observations are applicable for libvpx as well.",
